[WORK]
UID: WORK-ASSESS-AUTO-AUTHORSHIP
TITLE: Assess and select how authorship gets set automatically from the key
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
An assessment node, deliberately kept as one node because the operator framed
it as research carrying several non-exhaustive ideas rather than a settled
design:

(a) an identity-authorship path -- a daemon, scribe, LLM hooks, and jail.nix's
bubblewrap sandboxing enforcing access to the LLM's key, so that a write made
under that key carries the key's identity as authorship. Enforce, not make
impossible.

(b) a signing backstop -- GPG commit signing under the same jail-enforced key
access, plus a git hook and a CI check verifying that the authorship recorded
on every node touched by a commit matches that commit's signing key.

(c) "enforce, not make impossible" is a deliberate scope line: the future
beads-harness VM is where the impossible-to-bypass guarantee is meant to land,
not this work.

(d) an open, unconfirmed question -- whether sops carries special GPG support
scoped under a single key that could simplify (a) or (b).

MECH-AGENT-IDENTITY-KEYS already surveyed a per-agent-SSH-key design and
rejected it on exactly the ground jail.nix is aimed at: the agent runs as the
same UNIX user who owns the human's key, so a key the agent can reach is a key
the agent can substitute, and one machine under one user offers no principal
boundary. Bubblewrap-enforced access is what would give this a boundary that
node found missing. The work here is to assess these options and select.
<<<
